0 Monolith Assembly - 9-part stylized stack (7 Brick 1 x 4 + 1 Plate 1 x 4 + 1 Tile 1 x 4)
0 Name: monolith.ldr
0 Author: spex project (hand-authored placement data; not sourced from an official ldraw.org models/ release)
0 // Real stacking math: each part's own bottom surface lands exactly on
0 // the real top surface of everything stacked below it - translation_y
0 // = -(height_from_base + own_height), LDraw's own +Y-down convention.
0 // Brick 1x4 (3010.dat) is 24 LDU tall, Plate 1x4 (3710.dat) is 8 LDU
0 // tall. Total stack height: 7*24 + 2*8 = 184 LDU = 73.6mm, matching
0 // spex's own already-verified real monolith numbers (ratio 1:4:9.20).
0 //
0 // The top course is a TILE (2431.dat), not a plate. A tile is plate
0 // height - 8 LDU, the same as the 3710 it replaces - and has no studs,
0 // so the stack reads as one smooth slab from above rather than as a
0 // stack of parts with a lid. Swapping rather than adding is the whole
0 // point: adding a tenth part would take the height to 192 LDU and the
0 // ratio to 1:4:9.60, further from the 1:4:9 the object is quoting.
0 //
0 // 1:4:9 exactly is NOT constructible here, and the proof is short.
0 // It would need 180 LDU from 1x4 bricks and plates: 24a + 8b = 180,
0 // i.e. 6a + 2b = 45 - even on the left, odd on the right. No solution.
0 // The reachable heights bracket it symmetrically: 176 LDU is 1:4:8.80
0 // and 184 LDU is 1:4:9.20, both off by exactly 0.20. The grid cannot
0 // hit the ideal from either side, which is the piece's own subject
0 // rather than a defect in this file.
1 0 0 -24 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 3010.dat
1 0 0 -48 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 3010.dat
1 0 0 -72 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 3010.dat
1 0 0 -96 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 3010.dat
1 0 0 -120 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 3010.dat
1 0 0 -144 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 3010.dat
1 0 0 -168 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 3010.dat
1 0 0 -176 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 3710.dat
1 0 0 -184 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 2431.dat
